<?php
/**
 * Spartrak — the search results page, and its empty state.
 *
 * FIGMA SSOT: 1368:39180 ("نتائج البحث. لا يوجد بحث"), empty-state container
 * 1368:41143.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * WHY THIS TEMPLATE IS OVERRIDDEN AT ALL
 * ===========================================================================
 * Core's result.phtml is one if/else: results, or a `.message.notice` box
 * reading "Your search returned no results." That box is what this storefront
 * rendered, where Figma draws the shared empty state.
 *
 * There is no narrower extension point. The no-results branch is inline in
 * this template rather than a block, so layout XML cannot replace it, and the
 * design needs different MARKUP, not different words, so translation cannot
 * either. A theme override of a 42-line core template is the smallest correct
 * change — a theme file, not a `vendor/` edit (CLAUDE.md §2).
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * (a) THE RESULTS BRANCH IS CORE'S, AND NOTHING RUNS BEFORE IT
 * ===========================================================================
 * Everything from `if ($block->getResultCount())` to the `else` is core's
 * byte-for-byte, and the only statement above it is core's own `$productList`
 * assignment — deliberately, after the first version of this file took the
 * whole page down.
 *
 * WHAT HAPPENED, recorded because the failure mode is invisible: this template
 * read the search term from a layout argument,
 *
 *     <argument name="search_helper" xsi:type="object">Magento\Search\Helper\Data</argument>
 *
 * and hoisted four assignments above the branch to use it. Deployed, the whole
 * `content` container rendered EMPTY — no product grid on a search that HAD
 * hits, no breadcrumb, no page title — because
 * Magento\Framework\View\Layout::renderNonCachedElement() catches \Exception,
 * logs it and returns an empty string for that element, and a container whose
 * child throws loses ALL of its own output. One bad statement in this file
 * silently blanked the search page, with a 200 and no visible error.
 *
 * `Magento\Search\Helper\Data` was the cause: NO core layout in the codebase
 * passes a Helper as an `xsi:type="object"` block argument (verified by grep
 * across vendor/), and Layout\Argument\Interpreter\DataObject validates what
 * it instantiates against an expected class. The argument is gone and this
 * file now takes on no dependency at all.
 *
 * The lesson is kept in the structure: the empty branch is the only thing this
 * override owns, so nothing it needs is computed until that branch is taken. A
 * search that finds products cannot be affected by this file.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * (b) THE SEARCH TERM, AND ESCAPING — CLAUDE.md §17
 * ===========================================================================
 * Read from the request and escaped HERE with `$escaper->escapeHtml()`, so the
 * escaping is visible at the point of output rather than trusted from
 * somewhere upstream.
 *
 * `is_array` guarded: `?q[]=x` makes getParam() return an array, and an
 * unguarded array echoes as the string "Array". Core's own helper carries the
 * identical guard (Search\Helper\Data::getQueryText()), which is where this is
 * taken from.
 *
 * Trimmed to 128 characters — Magento's own `catalog/search/max_query_length`
 * default — so a pathological query cannot stretch the heading. When the term
 * was longer, core's note message already says it was shortened, and that
 * message is rendered below (see (c)).
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * (c) MAGENTO'S OWN MESSAGES ARE KEPT
 * ===========================================================================
 * Figma draws one sentence. Core's branch can carry two things that say
 * something the design's sentence does not:
 *
 *   getNoResultText()   "Minimum Search query length is 3" when the query was
 *                       too short — Block\Result::getNoResultText(). Also the
 *                       merchant's own configured no-result text.
 *   getNoteMessages()   "Your search query can't be longer than 128, so we
 *                       shortened your query" — Search\Helper\Data::checkNotes.
 *
 * Dropping them would leave a shopper whose query was too short reading "check
 * the spelling" and retyping the same two characters. They render as `__note`
 * paragraphs — this component's existing quieter aside, already used by the
 * minicart's empty drawer — beneath the design's sentence.
 *
 * `getAdditionalHtml()` is core's child-block slot; on this install it is the
 * search-terms-log block, which renders nothing visible. Kept regardless.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * PERFORMANCE / LCP
 * ===========================================================================
 * The LCP element is the 190px illustration, the only asset this state adds.
 * It is a CSS background on an aria-hidden span — the same mechanism as the
 * component's other callers — so its URL comes from LESS at compile time out
 * of a stylesheet the page already blocks on, not from a template at render
 * time. Not lazy-loaded: it is above the fold at every viewport, and its box
 * is reserved by the component's explicit 190x190, so it cannot shift layout.
 *
 * Inlining the SVG would add 78KB to an uncacheable HTML document on every
 * no-results view; as a file it is fetched once and cached for every empty
 * state the store shows (§13).
 *
 * @var \Magento\CatalogSearch\Block\Result $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */

$productList = $block->getProductListHtml();
?>
<?php if ($block->getResultCount()) : ?>
    <?= /* @noEscape */ $block->getChildHtml('tagged_product_list_rss_link') ?>
<div class="search results">
    <?php if ($messages = $block->getNoteMessages()) : ?>
    <div class="message notice">
        <div>
            <?php foreach ($messages as $message) : ?>
                <?= /* @noEscape */ $message ?><br />
            <?php endforeach; ?>
        </div>
    </div>
    <?php endif; ?>
    <?= /* @noEscape */ $productList ?>
</div>
<?php else : ?>
<?php
// See (b). Below the branch, so a search WITH results never runs any of it.
$queryText = $block->getRequest()->getParam('q');
$queryText = ($queryText === null || is_array($queryText)) ? '' : trim((string) $queryText);

if ($queryText !== '' && mb_strlen($queryText) > 128) {
    $queryText = mb_substr($queryText, 0, 128);
}

$noResultText = (string) $block->getNoResultText();
$noteMessages = $block->getNoteMessages();
?>
<div class="search results spartrak-empty-state spartrak-empty-state--nothing-found">
    <span class="spartrak-empty-state__art spartrak-empty-state__art--nothing-found" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    <div class="spartrak-empty-state__message">
        <div class="spartrak-empty-state__copy">
            <?php /*
                An <h1>, not the <strong> the component's other callers use. On
                those surfaces the page already has a heading above the empty
                state; here this sentence IS the page's heading.

                Core prints one too — `page.main.title`, reading
                "نتائج البحث عن: 'x'" — which Figma does not draw on this frame.
                It is hidden by components/_empty-state.less with
                `body.catalogsearch-result-index:has(.spartrak-empty-state--nothing-found)`, so it goes only on a
                page that rendered THIS state and a search that found something
                keeps its heading. That file records why the block is not
                removed server-side instead: doing so means asking the result
                block for its count during layout, which loads the product
                collection before the toolbar has set its sort order and page
                size.

                `display: none` takes core's heading out of the accessibility
                tree as well, so this h1 is the page's only heading for a
                screen reader rather than the second of two.
            */ ?>
            <h1 class="spartrak-empty-state__title">
                <?php if ($queryText !== '') : ?>
                    <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('No results for')) ?>
                    &ldquo;<?= $escaper->escapeHtml($queryText) ?>&rdquo;
                <?php else : ?>
                    <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('No results found')) ?>
                <?php endif; ?>
            </h1>

            <p class="spartrak-empty-state__text">
                <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('Please check the spelling and try again.')) ?>
            </p>

            <?php // See (c): what core would have said, kept. ?>
            <?php if ($noResultText !== '') : ?>
                <p class="spartrak-empty-state__note"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml($noResultText) ?></p>
            <?php endif; ?>

            <?php foreach ($noteMessages as $noteMessage) : ?>
                <p class="spartrak-empty-state__note"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml($noteMessage) ?></p>
            <?php endforeach; ?>
        </div>

        <a class="spartrak-empty-state__action spartrak-empty-state__action--primary"
           href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getBaseUrl()) ?>">
            <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('Go to homepage')) ?>
        </a>
    </div>
</div>
<?= /* @noEscape */ $block->getAdditionalHtml() ?>
<?php endif; ?>
